\section{Register Classes and Overlapping Names}

A register name, a hardware storage location, and an eligible operand
are different notions. In Smith, Ramsey, and Holloway's \emph{A
Generalized Algorithm for Graph-Coloring Register Allocation}, the
allocator accounts for register classes and aliases rather than treating
physical registers as interchangeable colors. One representation gives
each name $r$ a set of occupied hardware units $U(r)$. A value $v$
must choose from its eligible names $D_v$. If $v$ and $w$ hold
distinct live values at a point, the chosen names must have disjoint
unit sets. Instruction constraints can make $D_v$ smaller than the set
of names that can hold its type.

For example, four scalar names $s_0,\ldots,s_3$ occupy distinct units
$0,\ldots,3$. Two wide names $d_0$ and $d_1$ occupy
$\{0,1\}$ and $\{2,3\}$. If a live wide value uses $d_0$ and a
scalar operand must use either $s_0$ or $s_1$, both choices are
blocked; moving the wide value to $d_1$ frees both. A test of
``different names'' would accept $d_0$ together with $s_1$.
A test of ``at most two simultaneous values'' would fail to explain
the conflict. Even without aliases, three live values each eligible
for only $\{s_0,s_1\}$ cannot be assigned, although the target has
four scalar registers. Capacity by nominal register file is only a
necessary check. Conversely, partial writes can have effects beyond
the nominal written units; the target description must state which
units each instruction really reads, writes, and implicitly clobbers.

\section{Constraints at an Instruction Boundary}

Some requirements are relations between operands. A destructive
two-address operation producing $y$ from $x$ and $z$ requires its
first input and output to occupy the same location. When $x$ is dead
after the operation, direct reuse is possible if the eligible sets
permit it. If $x$ is still needed, lower to $t\gets x$ followed by
$t\gets t\mathbin{\mathrm{op}}z$ and let the result be $y$. The
allocator ties the short-lived first input to the output, not the
entire range of $x$ to the entire range of $y$. The copy can be
coalesced only when the resulting lifetimes and constraints permit it.
If the operation writes before reading another input, that input and
the output must also be treated as interfering during the operation,
even if the input dies there.

Fixed operands invite the same localization: copy an input into the
required register just before the instruction rather than assigning
that register to its entire range. If two uses require disjoint
register classes, intersecting their operand masks gives an empty
domain for the unsplit range, not an intrinsically impossible program.
Separate the uses with copies using legal transfer instructions,
possibly through a stack slot. Include implicit flags and hidden
scratch definitions in the instruction description. For a
regression test, hold a sentinel in one lane of a wide register,
execute a short instruction whose encoding has an implicit effect
on that lane, and read the sentinel afterward. The allocator must
move or save the sentinel or select a form without the clobber.
Testing only the instruction's explicit output misses the failure.

\section{Encoding Legal and Preferred Pairs}

Scholz and Eckstein's \emph{Register Allocation for Irregular
Architectures} formulates the problem with partitioned Boolean
quadratic programming. In finite-domain notation, minimize
\[
     \sum_v c_v(r_v)+\sum_{\{v,w\}} C_{vw}(r_v,r_w),
     \qquad r_v\in D_v .
\]
Unary costs penalize individual alternatives; pair costs can forbid
overlap with infinity or charge for an uncoalesced move. In the wide
example, suppose $d_0$ is free to choose in the absence of conflict
while $d_1$ requires a move of cost one. Both entries pairing $d_0$
with the scalar's two choices are infinite. Either scalar choice
paired with $d_1$ has zero pair cost, so the minimum feasible cost
is one. If a stack alternative is admitted, its unary cost must
represent an implementable spill and reload plan, not permission to
feed a stack slot to a register-only instruction.

Multiple simultaneous requirements can outgrow pair costs over values
alone: an instruction might have two alternative encodings, each with
a different admissible combination of operands. Model the encoding as
a decision as well, or lower to fixed legal forms before assigning
registers. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-239} develops the former strategy in a
joint model; the target vocabulary comes from
Chapter~\ref{ch:target-machines}.

