\section{Filter Encodings before Binding Operand Names}

A table of individual register domains can admit a combination that
no single encoding supports. Consider a toy target with two forms
of a destructive vector add. Both require an input tied to the output,
but form $L$ accepts only a low destination and a high untied
source, while form $H$ takes the reverse. A proposed assignment
may already come from an allocator; the following procedure checks
it rather than searching for a different assignment. Assume operand
positions are known and the table provides actual written units,
including implicit writes. The caller supplies the units occupied
by values that must survive the instruction, excluding its result.

\begin{sudocode}[Check complete forms, not projected domains]
\ForAll{forms $f$ of the selected operation}
  \If{some assigned name is absent from its domain in $f$}
    \State \textbf{continue} \Comment{Check within one form}
  \EndIf
  \If{a tie or early-write exclusion in $f$ fails}
    \State \textbf{continue}
  \EndIf
  \If{$\Call{Writes}{f,names}\cap keep\ne\emptyset$}
    \State \textbf{continue} \Comment{Implicit writes count too}
  \EndIf
  \State \Return $f$
\EndFor
\State \Return no legal form
\end{sudocode}

Let $v_0,v_1$ be low names and $v_2,v_3$ high names. With the tied
input holding 7 in $v_0$ and the other input holding 8 in $v_1$,
the pair is rejected even though the union of forms admits each name
at its position. Moving the untied input to $v_2$ yields form $L$
and a result of 15 in $v_0$. Now let $L$ also clear a status unit
$u$ whose value 3 is needed later. With $u$ in $keep$, that same
assignment is rejected. A test that observes only the sum would miss
the status loss.

The invariant is that every acceptance names a witness form that can
be emitted without changing the assignment. If no form works, ask
allocation or instruction selection to repair it. A bounded scan of
an opcode's form table is cheap enough to repeat as a validation step,
but its soundness still depends on accurate alias and write masks.
